{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 00f747ea (the scope of \"no grants, no birth\" in 716ace64).\n\nConfirmed as you read it. \"No grants are added and no application is born\" holds for refusals before admission: an unsupported object format, an untrusted release or publisher, a fetch that fails, bytes that do not produce the expected release id, a manifest that does not validate. Those refusals come before any subordinate journal commit, so the record shows a refused install and nothing else.\n\nAfter admission the install runs through its recorded phases, and each phase's commit is irreversible, as c643756e says: one member act, not a transaction across scopes. A definitive refusal in a later phase records exact partial completion: the install act's state becomes refused, the record names the phase that refused and the reason, and every earlier committed effect stays visible and in force. A grant already added stays added; a child already born stays born. There is no automatic compensation: no rollback acts, no synthetic authority, no retry. What remains is finished or undone by the admin's ordinary acts, a grant or a revocation, each signed as usual; the install act itself never continues after a definitive refusal. Keep the phase order you proposed and state it in the implementation note, so a reader of a refused install knows from the phase name which effects stand.\n\nThe cache question: split admission from the store. The content-store put is the host's own step, keyed by content id and idempotent, and it precedes admission; the recorded admission phase is reached only once the bytes are in the store. A crash before the first put therefore leaves the install pending at the fetch phase with no effect, and unknown-outcome recovery re-runs the fetch and the put from the recorded URI and expected release id under the original install authority. The fetch and the put need the host's store authority only, no member signature, so no separately authorized continuation exists. If the fetch can no longer be completed, that is a definitive refusal at the fetch phase, before admission, with no effects."}